    seg: [
      { t: "In June 2024 the Supreme Court ruled that possessing cannabis for personal use is not a crime, and set a presumption: up to 40 g counts as personal use. It is still prohibited — the cannabis is seized and the person receives an administrative measure such as a warning or a mandatory educational course. Police can still treat someone under 40 g as a dealer if there is other evidence, such as scales or packaging.", src: ["conectas", "sechat"] },
      { t: "The same ruling presumes that up to six female plants are for personal use, so growing them is an administrative infraction rather than a crime. It is not a right to grow: plants are seized. Some patients hold individual court orders allowing them to cultivate.", src: ["sechat", "gv"] },
      { t: "Any supply to another person is trafficking, a serious crime with a minimum of five years.", src: ["gv", "conectas"] },
      { t: "Use remains an unlawful act subject to administrative measures, not criminal ones. Public use still leads to police stops and seizure.", src: ["gv", "sechat"] },
      { t: "No adult-use sales. Pharmacies sell registered cannabis products on prescription, and patients can import with authorisation from the health regulator.", src: ["anvisa", "gv"] },
      { t: "In February 2026 the health regulator published rules allowing companies to cultivate low-THC cannabis (0.3% or less) for medical, pharmaceutical and research purposes under special authorisation, after a 2024 high-court order. No adult-use production.", src: ["anvisa"] },
      { t: "Prescription oils and extracts, mostly CBD-dominant; no legal flower for general sale.", src: ["anvisa"] },
      { t: "Doctors can prescribe registered or imported cannabis products; costs are high, and patient associations and court-authorised home growers fill part of the gap.", src: ["anvisa", "gv"] },
      { t: "Users are no longer criminally punished, but Brazil's drug law is harsh on anyone classed as a dealer, and critics note that the line is drawn unevenly, falling hardest on poor and Black Brazilians.", src: ["conectas", "gv"] },
      { t: "Administrative measures do not create a criminal record. The ruling opened the way to review past convictions for small amounts.", src: ["conectas"] },
    ],
